ABSTRACT = {
 "Aim": "To identify Korean districts where national health screening flags more low haemoglobin than expected for the sex and age of attendees, and the district characteristics that accompany it, with inference about districts rather than individuals.",
 "Methods": f"This ecological panel study used {N['exam_total']/1e6:.0f} million general health screening records of adults in {N['n_districts']} districts, 2011–2024. The screening flag (haemoglobin below 12.0 g/dL in men and below 10.0 g/dL in women) was first reconciled with published haemoglobin distributions. Sex- and age-standardised ratios were analysed with Bayesian spatiotemporal models and {NHYP} district characteristics, with period-restricted refits and negative-control exposures.",
 "Results": f"Provincial flag rates tracked the proportion of attendees below the thresholds, with nationwide steps in the ratio of flagged to below-threshold records in 2013, 2015 and 2018. Standardised ratios showed a persistent excess in the south and deficits in the capital region. Districts with a larger share of attendees with diagnosed diabetes had higher ratios in the principal model (rate ratio per standard deviation {M[DM]['IRR']:.2f}; 95% credible interval {M[DM]['lo']:.2f}–{M[DM]['hi']:.2f}) and in all period-restricted refits. Districts with more older adults living alone had higher ratios overall ({M[OLD]['IRR']:.2f}; {M[OLD]['lo']:.2f}–{M[OLD]['hi']:.2f}) but not credibly in 2018–2024 ({JW(OLD, 'J3_2018_24')[0]:.2f}; {JW(OLD, 'J3_2018_24')[1]:.2f}–{JW(OLD, 'J3_2018_24')[2]:.2f}). Physical activity showed no consistent association.",
 "Conclusions": "Screen-detected low haemoglobin follows a persistent southern geography in Korea. These district-level patterns locate areas for assessing the follow-up of abnormal screening results; they do not establish individual causes.",
}

INTRO = [
 f"Anaemia affected almost a quarter of the world's population in 2021, and its burden varies widely by age, sex and place [@gbd2023prevalence]. In older people, nutrient deficiency, chronic inflammation or renal disease, and unexplained anaemia each account for about one-third of cases [@guralnik2004prevalence], and anaemia is associated with a decline in physical performance [@penninx2003anemia]. In a Korean national survey, older adults with anaemia more often lived alone, received social assistance or had diabetes or chronic renal failure [@chueh2020high], and diabetes was associated with anaemia in Korean adults [@kim2021association]. These individual-level studies do not identify the districts in which screening flags more low haemoglobin than expected, which is what is needed to decide where follow-up of abnormal results should be strengthened.",
 f"Korea's National Health Insurance Service screens tens of millions of adults [@seong2017cohort], and the national statistics portal reports each year the number of screening records flagged as suspected anaemia in every district [@web:kosis2026screening]. District-level clustering of anaemia has been shown among rural men in India [@singh2024district], but routine haemoglobin screening of a national adult population appears not to have been used to map where low haemoglobin accumulates. Area characteristics plausibly matter: living alone is associated with malnutrition risk in older people [@besoramoreno2020social], food insecurity with micronutrient deficiency and anaemia [@lopes2023food], and nutrition-label reading with healthier micronutrient intake [@kim2014association], whereas smoking raises haemoglobin and can mask anaemia [@nordenberg1990effect].",
 f"This study treats the district, not the person, as the unit of inference, because district associations cannot be transferred to the people who live there [@morgenstern1995ecologic; @greenland1989ecological]. It asks where in Korea screening flags more low haemoglobin than expected for the sex and age of attendees, how stable that geography is, and which district characteristics accompany it. Because a screening flag is an administrative judgement, the flag was first reconciled with the published distribution of measured haemoglobin.",
]

METHODS = [
("Design, outcome and population",
 f"This ecological panel study used annual district statistics of the National Health Insurance Service general health screening programme [@seong2017cohort]: the number of screening records with a recorded judgement and the number flagged as suspected anaemia, by district and year and nationally by sex and age group [@web:kosis2026screening]. Under the Ministry of Health and Welfare's standards, haemoglobin below 12.0 g/dL in men and below 10.0 g/dL in women is judged as suspected anaemia; no other test enters this judgement, and high haemoglobin is not flagged [@web:mohw2024standards]. The threshold for women is lower than the 12.0 g/dL commonly used to define anaemia [@pasricha2018revisiting], so in women the flag excludes mild anaemia. Counts are screening records, not unique individuals. Districts were harmonised to 2024 boundaries; Sejong, reported separately in only 1 of 14 years, was excluded from estimation but kept as an unobserved district in the adjacency graph, leaving {N['n_districts']} districts and {N['n_rows']:,} district-years ({sup('methods')})."),
("Judgement check",
 f"Because the flag is an administrative judgement, the annual national and provincial numbers flagged in each sex were compared with the numbers of attendees whose measured haemoglobin fell below the same thresholds in the published haemoglobin distribution tables [@web:kosis2026hb]. A stable ratio indicates a stable aggregate relationship between flagged and below-threshold records, not exact agreement; a step indicates a discontinuity whose cause cannot be established from these data. Expected numbers based on the national positivity of each year account for such steps only to the extent that they applied similarly across districts ({sup('judgement')})."),
("Sex and age standardisation",
 f"Expected numbers flagged were obtained by indirect standardisation [@inskip1983methods]: national positivity in each year, sex and age group (19 years or younger, 5-year groups to 80–84 years, and 85 years or older) was applied to the estimated age structure of each district's attendees, derived from the resident population [@web:mois2026population] and national uptake by sex and age, and rescaled to the observed attendees of each sex. The standardised anaemia ratio is the observed over the expected number."),
("District characteristics",
 f"Characteristics and expected directions were fixed before any covariate model was fitted: basic livelihood security receipt (+) [@lopes2023food], tertiary education (−), nutrition-label literacy (−) [@kim2014association] and the share of households of older adults living alone (+) [@chueh2020high; @besoramoreno2020social]; marriages involving a foreign spouse (+) [@lee2019increased] and the total fertility rate (+) [@pasricha2021iron]; the share of attendees with diagnosed diabetes (+) [@thomas2003unrecognized; @kim2021association]; current smoking (−) [@nordenberg1990effect], high-risk drinking (+), walking and moderate-or-vigorous physical activity (−) from the Community Health Survey; and internal medicine specialists per 100,000 population (−) and screening uptake (−). Urban population share, physicians per 1,000 population, sex ratio, fiscal autonomy and the share aged 80 years or older were forced into every model. Diagnosed diabetes and living alone are crude shares, not age-standardised. Covariates were standardised to 1 standard deviation (SD). Living alone was observed from 2015, with district means filled for 2011–2014, and tertiary education was interpolated between the 2015 and 2020 censuses ({sup('covariates')}; checks in {sup('checks')})."),
("Statistical analysis",
 f"The likelihood (Poisson or negative binomial) was chosen by the widely applicable information criterion (WAIC). Models had an offset of log expected numbers, a Besag–York–Mollié 2 (BYM2) district effect with penalised-complexity priors [@riebler2016intuitive] and random-walk and independent year effects, fitted by integrated nested Laplace approximation [@rue2009approximate] in R 4.6.0. Rate ratios (RRs) per 1 SD are reported with 95% credible intervals (CrIs); an association was credible when the interval excluded 1. RRs are mutually adjusted ecological associations, not individual or causal effects [@morgenstern1995ecologic], and were interpreted as exploratory. The model was refitted with 200 random covariate subsets [@steegen2016increasing]; a covariate was subset-stable if credible in at least 75% of subsets containing it, with the same sign in at least 95%. Residual hot and cold spots had a posterior probability greater than 0.95 or less than 0.05 that the residual spatial RR exceeded 1, for the whole period and for 2011–2016 and 2017–2024."),
("Sensitivity analyses",
 f"Sensitivity analyses added Knorr-Held type I–IV space–time interactions [@knorrheld2000bayesian]; split subset-stable covariates into district means and within-district deviations [@bell2015explaining]; refitted the model in each window, with covariate-by-window interactions, and after excluding 2011–2012 (2013–2024) and separately for 2013–2017 and 2018–2024, either side of the largest step in the judgement ratio; recomputed expected numbers from residents instead of attendees; and varied the likelihood, priors, neighbourhoods, controls, lags, left-out provinces or years and filled values ({sup('specs')}). Treated gastric cancer and infectious disease served as comparison outcomes and hit-and-run accidents, fires and household waste recycling as negative-control exposures [@lipsitch2010negative]; E-values were computed [@vanderweele2017sensitivity]. Associations against the expected direction were examined post hoc ({sup('posthoc')})."),
("Ethics, reporting and use of artificial intelligence",
 "The Institutional Review Board of Hongik University approved the study (7002340-202511-HR-012; continuing review 7002340-202611-HR-040) and waived informed consent because only published aggregate statistics were used; the study followed the Declaration of Helsinki and the STROBE statement [@vonelm2007strengthening] (" + sup("strobe") + "). The author used Claude (Anthropic) and ChatGPT (OpenAI) only for translation and English-language editing of the manuscript; they were not used for study design, data analysis or interpretation, and the author checked all edited text and takes full responsibility for the content."),
]

