("Spatial pattern",
 f"Moran's I of observed log ratios was {N['moran_pooled']:.2f} pooled over years and {N['moran_year_min']:.2f}–{N['moran_year_max']:.2f} annually; the BYM2 spatial fraction was {N['phi'][0]:.2f} (95% CrI {N['phi'][1]:.2f}–{N['phi'][2]:.2f}). Residual RRs ranged from {N['rr_min']:.2f} to {N['rr_max']:.2f}, with {N['n_hot']} hot spots, mainly in {sidos(N['hot_by_sido'], 3)}, and {N['n_cold']} cold spots, mainly in {sidos(N['cold_by_sido'], 3)} (Figure 2A). Of the hot spots, {N['hot_gun']} were rural counties. In both windows, {N['hot_all_windows']} districts were hot spots and {N['cold_all_windows']} cold spots (Figure 2B), and district rankings were stable across years (Kendall's W {N['kendall_W']:.2f}) ({sup('maps')})."),
("District characteristics",
 f"The negative binomial likelihood was selected (WAIC {N['waic_nb']:,.1f} vs {N['waic_pois']:,.1f}). Adding the {NHYP} characteristics worsened WAIC from {N['waic_forced']:,.1f} to {N['waic_main']:,.1f}; residual Moran's I was {N['moran_resid']:.2f} (permutation <i>p</i> = {str(round(N['moran_resid_p'], 2)).lstrip('0')}). Six characteristics were credible, and four were subset-stable (Table 2). Standardised ratios were higher with a larger share of older adults living alone ({ci(OLD)}) and of attendees with diagnosed diabetes ({ci(DM)}), and lower with higher nutrition-label literacy ({ci(NUT)}). Against the expected direction, they were higher with higher screening uptake ({ci(UPT)}) and lower with more basic livelihood security receipt ({ci('basic_livelihood_rate')}) and a higher total fertility rate ({ci('ext__total_fertility_rate')}); the last two were credible in only {_pc('basic_livelihood_rate')} and {_pc('ext__total_fertility_rate')} of subsets. Walking ({ci('walking_practice_rate')}), moderate-or-vigorous activity ({ci('moderate_pa_rate')}), smoking, drinking, foreign-spouse marriages, tertiary education and internal medicine specialists were not credible. Of the negative-control exposures, hit-and-run accidents were not associated, whereas fires ({tci(_e('fires_per_10000'))}) and recycling ({tci(_e('waste_recycling_rate'))}) were."),
("Space–time models and sensitivity analyses",
 f"All four interaction types fitted better than the principal model (ΔWAIC {dW('KH_typeIII'):,.0f} to {dW('KH_typeII'):,.0f}, type II best). Living alone and diabetes remained credible in all four, with smaller RRs (type II {tci(S43('KH_typeII', OLD))} and {tci(S43('KH_typeII', DM))}); nutrition-label literacy was credible in types I, III and IV, uptake in types I and II only, and tertiary education became credible in types II and IV (Figure 3; Table 2). In window-specific models, living alone was credible in both windows, diabetes only in 2017–2024 ({tci(WI(DM, 'W2_2017-24'))}) and uptake only in 2011–2016 ({sup('windows')}). After excluding 2011–2012, diabetes was credible in 2013–2017 ({tci(JW(DM, 'J2_2013_17'))}), 2018–2024 ({tci(JW(DM, 'J3_2018_24'))}) and 2013–2024; living alone was credible in 2013–2017 and 2013–2024 but not in 2018–2024 ({tci(JW(OLD, 'J3_2018_24'))}); uptake was not credible in 2018–2024, and moderate-or-vigorous activity had a small positive association in 2018–2024 ({tci(JW('moderate_pa_rate', 'J3_2018_24'))}) ({sup('judgement')}). Across {ALT[OLD]['n_specs']} alternative fits, which exclude the period-restricted refits, living alone and diabetes were each credible in {ALT[OLD]['kept']}, nutrition-label literacy in {ALT[NUT]['kept']} and uptake in {ALT[UPT]['kept']}. When the share aged 80 years or older (variance inflation factor {N['vif_80']:.1f}; living alone {N['vif_old']:.1f}) was omitted, diabetes was not credible ({tci(SC('s2', DM))}). E-values were {N['evalue'][OLD]['E_point']:.2f} for living alone and {N['evalue'][DM]['E_point']:.2f} for diabetes. For treated gastric cancer, uptake ({tci(NCO('GCA', UPT))}) and diabetes ({tci(NCO('GCA', DM))}) were credible but living alone was not; for infectious disease, living alone and diabetes had RRs below 1 ({sup('specs')})."),
