DISCUSSION = [
 "After standardisation for sex and age, low haemoglobin flagged at screening showed a largely stable divide, with excess in the south-west and south-east, many hot spots being rural counties, and deficits in the capital region. Provincial flag rates tracked the proportion of attendees with haemoglobin below the published thresholds, although the ratio of flagged to below-threshold records shifted in nationwide steps, and the fall in positivity between 2016 and 2017 accompanied a smaller proportion below the thresholds rather than a change in that ratio. Districts with more attendees with diagnosed diabetes had more flagged low haemoglobin in most analyses; the larger association with the share of older adults living alone was not credible in 2018–2024; physical activity showed no consistent association.",
 "### Interpreting district-level associations",
 "These are statements about districts. They do not show that older people who live alone, or people with diabetes, are themselves more often anaemic, because district associations can arise from other residents and from district-level confounding [@morgenstern1995ecologic; @greenland1989ecological]. The flag also covers all adult ages, so district excess need not arise among older adults. The maps can locate districts in which the follow-up of abnormal haemoglobin results deserves assessment and generate hypotheses for individual-level studies; they do not establish unmet need or the effectiveness of additional follow-up.",
 "### Diabetes",
 "Districts with a larger share of attendees with diagnosed diabetes had higher ratios in the principal model, the interaction models and all period-restricted refits, although not in the 2011–2016 window, which includes the transitional years of judgement for men. Anaemia is common in diabetes, especially with albuminuria or reduced kidney function [@thomas2003unrecognized], and rises with the stage of chronic kidney disease [@stauffer2014prevalence]; renal insufficiency, inflammation and reduced erythropoietin production contribute in older people [@stauder2018anemia]. The association depended on adjustment for the highly collinear share aged 80 years or older and was also present, more weakly, for treated gastric cancer, so it may partly mark district differences in the health of attendees.",
 "### Older adults living alone",
 "The association with the share of older adults living alone was the largest in the principal model and persisted in all interaction models, but in 2018–2024 the estimate was small and not credible, so that period did not support a positive association. This share was observed only from 2015 and changes little within districts, which may add uncertainty in short periods, but the analysis does not establish why the estimate was attenuated. Living alone is associated with malnutrition risk in older people [@besoramoreno2020social], older Korean adults with anaemia more often live alone [@chueh2020high], and nutritional anaemia is associated with lower nutrient intake [@park2019comparison]; however, the screening statistics contain no information on diet, iron status or cause, and district associations cannot show that the people living alone were those flagged. Collinearity with the share of the oldest residents means that part of the association may reflect very old age not removed by standardisation. The small inverse association with nutrition-label literacy was no larger than those of negative-control exposures and cannot be distinguished from residual confounding.",
 "### Other characteristics",
 "District averages of self-reported physical activity may be too coarse to show an individual effect; the small positive association of moderate-or-vigorous activity in 2018–2024 ran against the expected direction and was not seen in the principal model. Smoking, expected to mask anaemia [@nordenberg1990effect], high-risk drinking and foreign-spouse marriages were not associated. Higher uptake was associated with more anaemia within districts over time, was shared by treated gastric cancer and was absent from 2018–2024, whereas district means ranked in the expected direction; this is compatible with changes in who attends as uptake rises, since adults of lower socioeconomic status attend check-ups less often [@shin2018association]. The unstable inverse association with basic livelihood security reversed its simple correlation, possibly through collinearity with living alone.",
 "### Strengths and limitations",
 "The study covered every harmonised district except Sejong over 14 years, standardised for sex and age within the screened population, and reconciled the screening flag with measured haemoglobin before modelling. The ratio of flagged to below-threshold records shifted in nationwide steps, in men in 2013 and in both sexes in 2015 and 2018; year-specific expected numbers account for these steps only if they applied similarly across districts, and why flagged records exceeded below-threshold records in women from 2018 could not be established. The outcome is a programme flag from a single haemoglobin measurement, without cause, severity or treatment. Records are not unique people, the attendee age structure was estimated, and age-specific district data were unavailable. Covariates differ in quality, living alone was filled for early years, ageing-related characteristics were collinear, and adding the district characteristics worsened predictive fit over the spatial and temporal effects, so they describe rather than explain the geography. The credible negative-control exposures and small E-values show that modest residual confounding could explain any of the associations.",
 "### Conclusion",
 "Low haemoglobin flagged at Korean health screening follows a persistent southern geography after sex and age are taken into account. At district level it is more common where many attendees have diabetes, an association that depended on adjustment for the share of the oldest residents, whereas its association with the share of older adults living alone was not credible in 2018–2024. These districts are candidates for assessing the follow-up of abnormal haemoglobin results, and the findings generate hypotheses for individual-level studies of diabetes, diet and iron status in older adults.",
]

# 문장 주장 대조(결과표와 어긋나면 빌드 중단)
assert N["n_cred_hyp"] == 6 and N["n_robust"] == 4 and N["waic_main"] > N["waic_forced"] and BEST == "KH_typeII" and N["waic_nb"] < N["waic_pois"]
assert M[OLD]["IRR"] > 1 and M[DM]["IRR"] > 1 and M[NUT]["IRR"] < 1 and M[UPT]["IRR"] > 1 and all(M[v]["cred"] for v in ROB)
assert all(M[v]["cred"] and M[v]["IRR"] < 1 for v in REV) and all(N["mv"][v]["cred_frac"] < 0.75 for v in REV)
assert not any(M[v]["cred"] for v in NULLV)
assert not N["negctl"]["hit_and_run_rate"]["cred"] and N["negctl"]["fires_per_10000"]["cred"] and N["negctl"]["waste_recycling_rate"]["cred"]
assert all(dW(a) < 0 for a in KH) and all(S43(a, v)[3] for a in KH for v in (OLD, DM))
assert [a for a in KH if S43(a, NUT)[3]] == ["KH_typeI", "KH_typeIII", "KH_typeIV"] and [a for a in KH if S43(a, UPT)[3]] == ["KH_typeI", "KH_typeII"]
assert all(WI(v, w)[3] for v in (OLD, NUT) for w in ("W1_2011-16", "W2_2017-24")) and not WI(DM, "W1_2011-16")[3] and WI(DM, "W2_2017-24")[3] and WI(UPT, "W1_2011-16")[3] and not WI(UPT, "W2_2017-24")[3]
assert all(N["epop"][v][3] for v in ROB) and N["epop"][UPT][0] > M[UPT]["IRR"]
assert not NCO("GCA", OLD)[3] and not NCO("GCA", NUT)[3] and NCO("GCA", UPT)[3] and NCO("GCA", UPT)[0] > 1 and NCO("GCA", DM)[3] and NCO("GCA", DM)[0] > 1 and NCO("INFECT", OLD)[3]
assert NCO("INFECT", OLD)[0] < 1 and NCO("INFECT", DM)[0] < 1
